  "slug": "promote-topic-0ce2",
  "summary": "New work links copper sensing, ER-mitochondria contacts, lipid tethers and VCP-dependent ER degradation to how organelles are remodeled under stress.",
  "synthesis": "The items share a focus on how organelles are remodeled or cleared under stress, each through a distinct route. In mitochondria, copper binding to OXA1L disrupts mitoribosome assembly and releases MRPL20 to the nucleus, activating ATF5-driven UPRmt that restrains cuproptosis. In yeast, the ER-vacuole tether Mdm1 balances phospholipid and neutral lipid metabolism to support autophagosome completion. ATAD1 at mitochondria-ER contacts moves mislocalized Pex26 to the ER, and induced ubiquitination degrades ER membrane proteins via VCP-dependent extraction, bypassing canonical ERAD. In Arabidopsis, AtNBR1 transiently associates with lipid droplets during germination, linked to seed longevity. The new element is assignment of specific sensors, tethers and extraction factors to each context rather than a single shared mechanism. Previous claims on iron-deficiency pexophagy, Rab32 and RAB11-RAB30 are not supported by these items.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
